'''Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit''' ('''CMRT'''), commonly refered to as simply the '''Castejón''' '''Metro,''' is a {{W|heavy rail}} {{W|elevated railway|elevated}} and {{W|Rapid transit|subway}} {{W|public transportation}} system serving the [[Castejón Metropolis]] in [[Balisca]]. CMRT serves [[List of Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit stations|326 stations]] along 22 routes on {{convert|764.4|km|sp=us}} of {{W|rapid transit}} lines. With an average of just under six million weekday passengers and just under three billion annual passengers in fiscal year 2023, CMRT is the [[List of Baliscan rapid transit systems by ridership|busiest heavy rail rapid transit system in Balisca]], and the x-[[List of metro systems|busiest in the world]]. It is complemented by the [[ComMuni]] system. 

The initial system opened in stages from 1909 to 1912 within the [[Ampurias]], [[Coruxón]] and [[Expansión]] boroughs as the ''Castejón Underground'' ("Subte"). It is operated by the [[Castejón Metropolitan Transit Authority]] which formed in 1922 following the [[W:Communization|communization]] of the Castejón Underground by the Metropolitan government. The system was extended most recently on 13 June 2020, when [[Civitas station|Civitas]] and [[Cuevas Bajas Station|Cuevas Bajas]] stations opened as part of the [[Concordia Rapid Transit]] system in partnership with the surrounding [[Alpujarra|Alpujarran]] operators and the [[Jatiel Transportation Authority|Jatiel Transit Authority]] (JTA).

The system has operated {{W|24/7}} service every day of the year throughout most of its history, barring emergencies and extreme weather events. By annual ridership, the Castejón Metropolitan Rapid Transit system is the busiest rapid transit system in both the {{W|Western Hemisphere|Western}} and the {{W|Southern Hemisphere}}s, as well as the [[W:List of metro systems|fourth-busiest rapid transit rail system in the world]]. In 2023, CMRT delivered over 3 billion rides, averaging approximately 10.9 million daily rides on weekdays and a combined 6 million rides each weekend (3.2 million on Saturdays, 2.8 million on Sundays). On 23 December 2023, more than 11 million people rode the metro system, establishing the highest single-day ridership since ridership was regularly monitored beginning in 1965.

Today, the network is one of the world's largest and most efficient urban railway systems, with the addition of {{W|wireless}} {{W|internet}} service on all trains; and {{W|platform screen doors}} at almost all stations. 

